# ESP-IDF build output & downloaded components (regenerated by idf.py)
# build.<target>/ too: one project now builds for two boards, each into its own directory.
build/
build.dial/
build.pro/
managed_components/
.cache/
# dependencies.lock is TRACKED (2026-09-17): it is the exact component set the release was built and
# flashed with. Ignoring it let every fresh checkout resolve the manifest anew — and the day LVGL 9.6.0
# landed on the registry, a fresh build stopped booting. Regenerate it on purpose with
# `idf.py update-dependencies`, and commit the change with the reason.

# Generated config. sdkconfig.defaults is the tracked source of truth; CMakeLists.txt regenerates
# sdkconfig whenever the defaults hash changes (see the comment there), leaving the old one as
# sdkconfig.superseded.
# …and the per-target names the two-board split produced. sdkconfig.defaults + the per-target
# sdkconfig.defaults.esp32s3 / .esp32p4 stay TRACKED — they are the source of truth; everything
# generated from them does not.
sdkconfig
sdkconfig.old
sdkconfig.superseded
sdkconfig.defaults.stamp
sdkconfig.dial
sdkconfig.pro
sdkconfig.dial.superseded
sdkconfig.pro.superseded
sdkconfig.dial.defaults.stamp
sdkconfig.pro.defaults.stamp
build-arm

# Local pairing secrets (WiFi password / API key) — do not commit.
# Copy main/provisioned_config.h.example → main/provisioned_config.h and fill in.
# The .local variant is a per-machine override that also carries secrets.
main/provisioned_config.h
main/provisioned_config.h.local
build-prod/

# Design mockups: HTML frames used to agree on a layout BEFORE writing LVGL code. They are a
# conversation, not a build input — nothing here compiles or ships.
mockup/
build-probe/
